<div style="border-bottom:2px solid var(--c-text);padding-bottom:10px;margin-bottom:1.25rem;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:baseline;"><div style="font-family:var(--font-serif);font-size:24px;font-weight:500;letter-spacing:-0.01em;">AI coding weekly</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--c-text2);font-family:var(--font-mono);">EDITION 17 &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; 2026-06-29 – 2026-07-06</div></div>
<div style="background:var(--c-bg2);border-left:3px solid var(--c-info);padding:14px 18px;margin-bottom:1.75rem;border-radius:0 6px 6px 0;"><div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:500;color:var(--c-info);letter-spacing:0.12em;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:6px;">TL;DR &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; The story so far</div><div style="font-family:var(--font-serif);font-size:15px;line-height:1.6;color:var(--c-text);font-style:italic;">Seventeen weeks in, the AI coding tools story has moved through four distinct chapters: is the code any good, is the agent safe, who governs the gate, and — this week — who pays, at what tier, with what evidence. The dominant pattern that ties them all together is that every question we thought was about the tools turns out to be about the humans doing the buying, evaluating, and reviewing.</div></div>
<div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:500;color:var(--c-warn);letter-spacing:0.1em;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:6px;">This week&rsquo;s big story</div>
<div style="font-family:var(--font-serif);font-size:28px;font-weight:500;line-height:1.2;color:var(--c-text);margin-bottom:12px;">The 18-day Anthropic AI lockout ends — with a $149.25 receipt attached</div>
<div style="font-family:var(--font-serif);font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--c-text);margin-bottom:14px;">On <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/news/redeploying-fable-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--c-text);text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:var(--c-bord2);"><strong>2026-07-01 the US Commerce Department reversed the June 12 export-control directive</strong></a> that had suspended worldwide access to Anthropic's most capable coding models. Claude Fable 5 was globally available again the same day on Claude Platform, Claude.ai, Claude Code, and Claude Cowork, with <strong>50% Max-plan usage inclusion through 2026-07-07</strong>. Anthropic acknowledged it has been "working closely with the US government since June 12"; <a href="https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/7/1/us-lifts-restrictions-on-powerful-ai-models-fable-mythos-anthropic-says" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--c-text);text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:var(--c-bord2);"><strong>Al Jazeera framed the resolution</strong></a> as an 18-day full lockout ending after Anthropic was approved to provide Mythos to US organizations "operating and defending critical infrastructure." Not a clean win, though: <a href="https://www.coindesk.com/tech/2026/07/01/anthropic-restores-ai-models-fable-mythos-after-the-u-s-lifts-export-controls" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--c-text);text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:var(--c-bord2);"><strong>CoinDesk preserves the fine print</strong></a> — Commerce reserved the right to "reevaluate" — and a <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48740771" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--c-text);text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:var(--c-bord2);"><strong>Hacker News thread</strong></a> split down the middle over whether the whole 18-day suspension was a real jailbreak response or security theater.</div>
<div style="font-family:var(--font-serif);font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--c-text);margin-bottom:14px;">Meanwhile, <a href="https://simonwillison.net/2026/Jul/5/sqlite-utils-fable/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--c-text);text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:var(--c-bord2);"><strong>Simon Willison landed a real number</strong></a> on the question that has been dominating the pricing discourse for weeks: is a top-tier Claude Fable subscription actually worth it? Willison used Fable to push his sqlite-utils library from 4.0alpha to 4.0rc2 — <strong>37 prompts, 34 commits, code changes across 30 files, $149.25 total</strong> — with Fable identifying <strong>5 release-blocker issues</strong> during self-review, one of them a critical data-loss bug in `delete_where()`. He upgraded from the $100/mo to the $200/mo Max plan to squeeze more Fable time in before the <strong>July 7 amnesty ends</strong> and Fable moves back to full API rates.</div>
<div style="font-family:var(--font-serif);font-size:16px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--c-text);margin-bottom:14px;">For the first time in weeks, the AI-coding pricing story has a defensible positive data point — not a horror story like Uber's four-month budget burn, but a real OSS release delivered for less than the cost of a mid-range laptop. It changes the burden of proof on the next FinOps complaint. But the reprieve is temporary: on <strong>July 7 Fable moves off Max-plan inclusion and back to full API rates</strong>, and the same practitioner community will find out whether the value proposition survives. <strong>Watch for:</strong> whether anyone can reproduce Willison-scale value at post-amnesty pricing, or whether we're back to the "AI is too expensive" narrative by next Monday.</div>
